@node Session identity
@section Session identity

@findex agent-session
@findex agent-session-buffer-name
Every live session buffer carries a buffer-local @code{agent-session} struct, captured once when the session starts. Its slots are @code{backend} (the backend symbol), @code{account} (the account name, or nil for the default), @code{directory} (the abbreviated project directory), @code{instance} (the instance name, or nil), and @code{id} (the CLI session id, or nil until known). The function @code{agent-session} returns the struct for a buffer, and @code{agent-session-buffer-name} derives the canonical buffer name from the identity. All orchestration code reads identity from this struct; nothing parses buffer names.

@findex agent-session-buffers
@vindex agent-session-id-functions
The @code{id} slot holds the backend's native session id and is maintained for the whole session lifetime: a non-fork resume seeds it in @code{agent-start-session}, the Claude backend records the @code{session_id} reported by every status poll (so a branch switch, which changes the id, updates it), and the Codex backend records the id @code{codex-session-identity} reports on every CLI hook event. A fresh session therefore acquires its id within one status poll interval (Claude) or at its first hook event or prompt submission, whichever comes first (Codex); a fresh app-server Codex session knows its thread id before any CLI hook event fires, so the submission hook is the earliest per-buffer signal. Whenever the recorded id changes, the abnormal hook @code{agent-session-id-functions} runs with the session buffer, so optional integrations — such as the Agent Log bridge — can observe identity without reading private variables. The public surface for such integrations is @code{agent-session}, the @code{agent-session-id} accessor, @code{agent-session-buffers} (all live session buffers across backends), and @code{agent-session-display-state}.

@node Session state machine
@section Session state machine

@findex agent-session-event
Each session buffer tracks its readiness in the buffer-local variable @code{agent--session-state}, which is one of @code{unknown}, @code{busy}, @code{awaiting-input}, or @code{closing}. Buffers start as @code{unknown} and stay there until a session event arrives, so a state that was never observed is never presented as one that was; the session switcher renders such sessions with @code{agent-unknown} and @code{agent-jump-to-waiting} skips them. All transitions go through @code{agent-session-event}, which accepts the events @code{stop}, @code{idle-prompt}, @code{blocked}, @code{submit}, @code{activity}, and @code{exit-request}. An @code{activity} event marks the session busy on evidence that it is working. It exists because Claude Code publishes no turn-start hook, so turns the user did not type — one driven by remote control, a scheduled task, or a resumed background job — would otherwise be unobservable, leaving the session displayed as waiting while it works. Claude's status line reports a fresh @code{prompt_id} for every turn whatever its origin, and @code{agent-claude--read-status} already polls that file for the modeline, so @code{agent-claude--detect-turn-start} emits @code{activity} when the identifier changes. No extra hook or configuration is needed. Detection latency is one poll interval, @code{agent-claude-status-interval}. A turn shorter than that interval both begins and ends between polls, and its @code{stop} event has already landed by the time the change is seen, so that case is detected and skipped rather than stranding the session as busy. A @code{blocked} event marks the session as waiting on the user without firing a ready alert, and is used for permission and elicitation dialogs: Claude reaches those from inside a turn with a tool call still in flight, so nothing else reports them as waiting even though they proceed only once the user answers. Backends translate raw CLI notifications into these events and never set session state directly. A @code{stop} or @code{idle-prompt} event first offers the transition to the before-exit chain; when the chain consumes it, ready alerts and display refreshes are suppressed. Ready alerts fire only for @code{idle-prompt} events, and a @code{submit} event delivered while the session is already busy is ignored, because backend submission hooks can fire more than once per submission.

@node Session teardown and leak policy
@section Session teardown and leak policy

Session resource cleanup has a single owner: @code{agent--session-teardown} runs a session buffer's @code{agent--teardown-functions} exactly once, whether the buffer is killed first or the CLI process exits first, then releases the session's home-row key. Backends and features register their per-session resources (status poll timers, monet servers, before-exit chains) as teardown functions instead of installing their own kill-buffer hooks. Claude buffers also adopt any already-running Monet server when @code{agent-claude-mode} is enabled or reloaded, so teardown owns sessions that predate the current Agent code. The monet websocket server needs one extra guard: when the Claude client disconnects, @code{monet--on-close-server} drops the session from monet's own table before teardown can look it up, so an @samp{:after} advice closes the listening server at that moment rather than letting it escape to the GC safety net. Resources that escape teardown are not silently reaped: safety nets report each one through a @samp{Warnings} buffer entry tagged @samp{agent}, naming the leaked resource. A leak warning therefore indicates a lifecycle bug worth reporting, not routine noise. The Monet GC sweep identifies servers by ownership, not by process name: Agent registers every server process it sees monet create or adopt, and the sweep only ever reaps registered servers that have lost their monet session. Websocket servers belonging to other packages (atomic-chrome, org-roam-ui) share the generic @samp{websocket server on port N} process name but are invisible to the sweep and can never be reported or killed as monet leaks.

@node Account model
@section Account model

@findex agent-account-select
@findex agent-account-init
Multi-account state lives in @samp{agent-account.el} and is keyed by backend. Each backend declares an accounts alist mapping account names to per-account configuration homes (@code{agent-claude-accounts}, @code{agent-codex-accounts}), a persisted selection file that survives Emacs restarts (@code{agent-claude-account-file}, @code{agent-codex-account-file}), and the environment variable that points the CLI at the selected home: @samp{CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR} for Claude Code and @samp{CODEX_HOME} for Codex. Account homes keep credentials separate, while shared state (settings, hooks, skills, sessions, history) is symlinked from the backend's canonical home so it remains available after switching accounts; conflicting account-local files are backed up to timestamped @samp{*.agent-backup-*} siblings first. @code{agent-account-select} persists a choice and syncs the account home, and @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs a home. When the merge engine reads a home's @samp{.claude.json}, a parse failure is retried once after a short delay before warning, since a running CLI rewrites the file frequently and a read can land mid-write. The account a session was started under is recorded in its @code{agent-session} struct, so restart and handoff preserve it even after the global selection changes.

@node Backend minor modes
@section Backend minor modes

@findex agent-claude-mode
@findex agent-codex-mode
The global minor modes @code{agent-claude-mode} and @code{agent-codex-mode} own every hook, advice, timer, and notification handler their backend installs. Loading @samp{agent-claude.el} or @samp{agent-codex.el} installs nothing; the modes must be enabled explicitly. Disabling a mode removes its hooks and advice symmetrically and restores the backend's previous notification function, leaving running sessions untouched. A typical setup:

@lisp
(use-package agent
  :config
  (require 'agent-claude)
  (require 'agent-codex)
  (require 'agent-chief)
  (agent-claude-mode 1)
  (agent-codex-mode 1))
@end lisp

