@vindex agent-act-on-slack-message-model
@vindex agent-act-on-slack-message-backend
@vindex agent-epoch-project-registry-file
@vindex agent-epoch-projects-root
@vindex agent-prompt-capture-directory
@vindex agent-prompt-capture-auto-save-delay
@vindex agent-sync-theme
@vindex agent-sigwinch-delay
@vindex agent-session-annotation-max-width
The user option @code{agent-protect-buffers} is a boolean that defaults to @code{t}. When enabled, killing a live AI session buffer prompts for confirmation through @code{agent-protect-buffer}. Buffers whose processes have already exited are allowed to die without the prompt.

The user option @code{agent-alert-on-ready} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled, backend notification handlers can call @code{agent-notify} to emit visual and/or audible alerts when a session is waiting for input. Codex's own terminal-bell notification path is routed through the same alert setting. @code{agent-alert-style} selects @code{visual}, @code{sound}, or @code{both}; it defaults to @code{both}. @code{agent-alert-sound} defaults to @code{nil}; set it to a sound file to enable sound alerts. @code{agent-alert-sound-player} optionally names an external sound player when Emacs sound support is unavailable.

The user option @code{agent-backtrace-file} controls where @code{agent-save-backtrace} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace} write Emacs backtraces. It defaults to a file in @code{temporary-file-directory}.

The unified orchestration commands share one set of options across backends. @code{agent-handoff-files} maps backend symbols to the handoff files read by @code{agent-handoff}. @code{agent-audit-skills} lists the audit skills run by @code{agent-audit-project}, and @code{agent-audit-project-directories} stores completion candidates for the audit directory prompt. @code{agent-debug-backtrace-model} and @code{agent-debug-backtrace-backend} configure the @samp{gptel} request used by @code{agent-debug-backtrace} to identify likely culprit packages from an Emacs backtrace. @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-model} and @code{agent-act-on-slack-message-backend} configure the @samp{gptel} request used by @code{agent-act-on-slack-message} to select an Epoch project from the Slack message at point.

The user options @code{agent-epoch-project-registry-file} and @code{agent-epoch-projects-root} configure Slack-message project routing. Both default to nil; Slack routing is unavailable until they point at a JSON project registry and the root directory containing the canonical project files.

The user option @code{agent-prompt-capture-directory} controls where @code{agent-capture-prompt} stores per-session Org files. Each file is keyed by backend, account, project directory, and session instance, so prompts are linked to the session they target and remain available after Emacs restarts. @code{agent-prompt-capture-auto-save-delay} controls the idle delay before capture buffers are saved automatically.

The user option @code{agent-sync-theme} is a boolean that defaults to @code{nil}. When enabled, theme changes in Emacs are persisted through each registered backend's theme-sync handler. This updates configuration files for new or restarted CLI sessions without sending theme commands into active terminal conversations.

The user option @code{agent-sigwinch-delay} is a number of seconds and defaults to @samp{0.5}. It controls how long @code{agent-fix-rendering} waits before sending @samp{SIGWINCH} to a terminal process. Increase it if an AI CLI reads the terminal size before the @samp{eat} window has settled.

The user option @code{agent-session-annotation-max-width} caps the width of session annotations in the session switcher (@ref{Shared commands}). It defaults to @code{nil}, which applies no cap beyond the fit to the frame. An integer narrows annotations to at most that many columns; it never widens them past what the frame holds, since the frame fit applies either way. Text longer than the resulting width is truncated with an ellipsis.

Snippet helpers use @samp{yasnippet} and @samp{consult-yasnippet} only when those optional packages are loaded.

@node Claude Code backend
@section Claude Code backend

@vindex agent-claude-warn-kill-with-branches
@vindex agent-claude-fork-worktree-directory
@vindex agent-claude-log-directory
@vindex agent-claude-status-interval
@vindex agent-claude-usage-interval
@vindex agent-claude-usage-max-interval
@vindex agent-claude-accounts
@vindex agent-claude-account-file
@vindex agent-claude-settings-file
@vindex agent-claude-hook-wrapper
@vindex agent-claude-statusline-script
@vindex agent-claude-status-directory
@vindex agent-claude-batch-allowed-tools
@vindex agent-claude-batch-permission-mode
@vindex agent-claude-batch-max-turns
@vindex agent-claude-batch-system-prompt
@vindex agent-claude-batch-model
@vindex agent-claude-org-todo-in-progress-keyword
The Claude backend options control account selection, session metadata, non-interactive execution, and branch workflows. @code{agent-claude-accounts} is an alist of account names to Claude configuration directories, and @code{agent-claude-account-file} stores the selected account across Emacs sessions. Use @code{agent-claude-select-account} or the account infix in @code{agent-menu} to switch accounts before starting a new session. Both commands are thin wrappers around @code{agent-account-select}, which persists the choice and syncs the account's config home; @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs any backend's account home.

Status and usage polling are controlled by integer intervals. @code{agent-claude-status-interval} defaults to @samp{5} seconds and reads status JSON emitted by the bundled statusline script. @code{agent-claude-usage-interval} defaults to @samp{300} seconds and backs off up to @code{agent-claude-usage-max-interval}, which defaults to @samp{900} seconds. Usage polling reads the account's OAuth token from the macOS Keychain, so accounts authenticated with an API key rather than an OAuth subscription—whose OAuth Keychain store is an empty JSON object—are silently skipped rather than treated as a broken lookup. The Keychain lookup runs from the temporary-file directory, so a poll that fires while the current buffer's @samp{default-directory} names a deleted worktree cannot break the subprocess spawn.

Batch execution options are passed to @samp{claude -p}. @code{agent-claude-batch-permission-mode} defaults to @samp{"auto"}, @code{agent-claude-batch-max-turns} defaults to @samp{30}, and the allowed tool, model, and appended-system-prompt options let you narrow or expand what non-interactive Claude tasks may do.

Branch and buffer safety options default conservatively. @code{agent-claude-warn-kill-with-branches} defaults to @code{t} and warns before killing a session that belongs to a branch tree. @code{agent-claude-fork-worktree-directory} defaults to a cache directory so isolated branch worktrees do not interfere with cloud sync.

Claude hook setup is explicit. @code{agent-claude-settings-file} names the settings file updated by @code{agent-claude-setup-config} and the individual ensure commands. @code{agent-claude-hook-wrapper} points to the upstream @samp{claude-code} hook wrapper, while @code{agent-claude-statusline-script} points to the bundled statusline helper. @code{agent-claude-status-directory} controls where the helper writes per-buffer status JSON files. The statusline setup refreshes an existing agent-owned @samp{claude-code-statusline.sh} command, which keeps the settings file correct after the package moves or is renamed, while preserving unrelated custom Claude statusline commands. Loading the backend does not modify Claude Code configuration.

@node Codex backend
@section Codex backend

@vindex agent-codex-accounts
@vindex agent-codex-account-file
@vindex agent-codex-skill-directories
@vindex agent-codex-exec-approval-policy
@vindex agent-codex-exec-sandbox-mode
@vindex agent-codex-exec-skip-git-repo-check
The Codex backend options control account selection, skill discovery, and non-interactive execution. @code{agent-codex-accounts} is an alist of account names to Codex home directories, and @code{agent-codex-account-file} stores the selected account across Emacs sessions. Account homes keep credentials such as @samp{auth.json} separate, while shared Codex state such as @samp{config.toml}, @samp{hooks.json}, skills, sessions, and @samp{history.jsonl} is symlinked from @samp{~/.codex/} so hooks, skills, project trust, and chat history remain available after switching accounts; the Claude backend shares state between account homes the same way. When an account-local file conflicts with a shared item, it is backed up to a timestamped @samp{*.agent-backup-*} sibling before the symlink is created, so no local state is silently lost. Use @code{agent-codex-select-account} or the Codex account infix in @code{agent-menu} to switch accounts before starting a new session. Both commands are thin wrappers around @code{agent-account-select}, which persists the choice and syncs the account's config home; @code{agent-account-init} prepares or repairs any backend's account home.

@code{agent-codex-skill-directories} is a list of additional directories scanned for Codex skills. The backend also scans standard Codex skill locations and project-local skill directories.

@code{agent-codex-exec-approval-policy}, @code{agent-codex-exec-sandbox-mode}, and @code{agent-codex-exec-skip-git-repo-check} configure the non-interactive Codex calls used by skill and audit workflows. The approval policy is passed as a top-level Codex CLI option before the @samp{exec} subcommand, while the sandbox and repository-check options are passed to @samp{codex exec} itself.

The @samp{gptel} classifier requests used by backtrace debugging and Slack routing ignore reasoning-block callback events and parse only the final response text. Backtrace debugging sends the model a truncated excerpt of the backtrace rather than the full contents: each line is cut at 400 characters and the excerpt is capped at 100,000 characters, since backtraces can embed multi-megabyte printed objects that exceed API payload limits, while the frame names at the start of each line suffice to identify packages. The agent session itself still receives the path to the complete backtrace file.

@node Chief-of-staff loop
@section Chief-of-staff loop

@vindex agent-chief-backend
@vindex agent-chief-mode
@vindex agent-chief-interval
@vindex agent-chief-directory
@vindex agent-chief-session-instance-name
@vindex agent-chief-state-file
@vindex agent-chief-context-functions
@vindex agent-chief-state-max-chars
@vindex agent-chief-max-turns
@vindex agent-chief-notify-function
@vindex agent-chief-record-model-state-updates
@vindex agent-chief-system-prompt
The optional @samp{agent-chief} module runs a periodic, auditable chief-of-staff workflow. Emacs owns the schedule, explicit state, and notifications. In the default @samp{session} mode, the selected backend is an ordinary long-lived Claude Code or Codex session that receives heartbeat prompts and remains available for normal conversation.

@code{agent-chief-backend} selects @samp{codex} or @samp{claude-code}. @code{agent-chief-mode} defaults to @samp{session}; set it to @samp{stateless} to use the older one-shot JSON-decision path. @code{agent-chief-interval} controls the timer interval in seconds. @code{agent-chief-directory} is the working directory for chief sessions and one-shot calls; by default it uses @samp{/Users/pablostafforini/My Drive/Epoch/} when that directory exists, falling back to @code{user-emacs-directory} otherwise. @code{agent-chief-session-instance-name} defaults to @samp{chief} and is used as the instance name for the dedicated interactive session.

@code{agent-chief-state-file} is an Org file used as the durable, inspectable state store; @samp{agent-chief} never writes opaque memory. Day plans and manual notes are appended there and, when a chief session is live, submitted into that session as conversational context.

State context treats only a @samp{Plan YYYY-MM-DD} heading dated today as current. Older plan headings are supplied as historical context, not active obligations, unless restated in today's plan.

@code{agent-chief-record-model-state-updates} controls whether model-proposed state updates from stateless ticks are appended to the state file. It defaults to nil because periodic model observations are not user-approved durable memory. Use explicit notes and day plans for ordinary state updates.

@code{agent-chief-context-functions} is the extension point for agenda, task, calendar, project, or custom status snippets. Each function is called with no arguments and should return a string. Nil and empty strings are ignored. The state file is included separately, truncated to @code{agent-chief-state-max-chars} characters. @code{agent-chief-max-turns} limits Claude Code ticks; Codex ticks use the existing @samp{codex exec} settings from @samp{agent-codex}.

In session mode, @code{agent-chief-start-session} sends only a short conversational kickoff asking the model to ask for today's plan; it does not dump the state file or stateless JSON contract into the visible transcript. Heartbeat prompts ask the model to respond exactly @samp{No nudge.} when no contact is warranted, or @samp{Nudge: MESSAGE} when it should alert the user. The backend's ordinary notification event tells @samp{agent-chief} when the response is complete; @samp{agent-chief} then inspects the session text since the heartbeat and calls @code{agent-chief-notify-function} only for a nudge. The default notification function logs the message and uses @samp{alert.el} when available. @code{agent-chief-system-prompt} is the standing instruction that makes the loop selective and forbids inventing obligations not present in the supplied context.

In stateless mode, backend output may include echoed CLI transcript text before the answer. The chief loop scans that output and uses the last valid JSON object with a @samp{notify} field as the decision, so echoed response-schema examples do not confuse the parser.

