@node Backend registration and display
@section Backend registration and display

@findex agent-register-backend
@findex agent-start-session
@findex agent-backend-icon-string
@findex agent-svg-icon
@findex agent-display-name
@findex agent-parse-skill-frontmatter
@findex agent-notify
@findex agent-protect-buffer
@findex agent-kill-session-buffer
The function @code{agent-register-backend} registers an AI tool by constructing an @code{agent-backend} struct from keyword arguments. Backend modules call it at load time. Keywords are validated against the struct's slot names, and the required slots @code{:buffer-p}, @code{:find-all-buffers}, and @code{:start-session} are checked so the shared session layer can rely on a consistent interface.

The full slot set is: identity and presentation (@code{name}, @code{label}, @code{icon}, @code{program}), buffer discovery (@code{buffer-p}, @code{find-all-buffers}, @code{find-buffers-for-dir}), session lifecycle (@code{start-session}, @code{session-identity}, @code{restart-options}), send primitives (@code{send-string}, @code{send-return}, @code{submit}), session state probes (@code{waiting-p}, @code{busy-p}, @code{background-tasks-p}, @code{duration-ms}, @code{display-name-suffix}, @code{notify}), accounts (@code{account-env-var}, @code{accounts}, @code{account-file}, @code{shared-config-items}, @code{canonical-home}, @code{account-init}), skills (@code{run-prompt}, @code{skill-roots}, @code{skill-command-prefix}), integration hooks (@code{sync-theme}, @code{menu-suffixes}), and exit safety (@code{before-exit-ready-to-close-p}, @code{before-kill-check}).

The @code{:start-session} slot starts a new session parameterized by an @code{agent-session} identity struct, optionally with an initial prompt or a resume id. The @code{:session-identity} slot returns a live session buffer's CLI session id, used by @code{agent-restart} to resume in place. @code{agent-start-session} is the core dispatcher that starts a session through its backend's @code{:start-session} function, passing any extra backend-specific options through. The @code{:send-string} slot inserts text into a session's prompt without submitting it, @code{:send-return} submits the pending prompt, and @code{:submit} atomically inserts and submits; the core wrappers @code{agent-send-string}, @code{agent-send-return}, and @code{agent-submit} dispatch through these slots and emit the corresponding session events.

@code{agent-display-name} returns a human-readable label for a session buffer. It starts with the project name, adds an instance name when needed to disambiguate duplicates, and lets backends append their own suffixes. The Claude backend uses this to expose branch identity in the modeline and switcher.

@code{agent-parse-skill-frontmatter} reads @samp{SKILL.md} frontmatter shared by Claude and Codex skill runners. It recognizes names, descriptions, argument hints, argument sources, argument choices, defaults, multiplicity, user-invocable flags, and model overrides.

@code{agent-svg-icon} renders SVG strings as inline images, using the current face foreground when the SVG uses @samp{currentColor}. Backends use this for modeline and transient display and can fall back to text abbreviations when SVG rendering is unavailable.

@node Modeline and status helpers
@section Modeline and status helpers

@findex agent-claude-status-model
@findex agent-claude-status-effort
@findex agent-claude-status-cost
@findex agent-claude-status-context-percent
@findex agent-claude-status-token-count
@findex agent-claude-status-duration-ms
@findex agent-claude-status-session-usage
@findex agent-claude-status-weekly-usage
@findex agent-codex-status-model
@findex agent-codex-status-effort
@findex agent-codex-status-duration-ms
The Claude and Codex status helpers are intended for modeline integrations such as @samp{doom-modeline}. Claude status functions read the current session status JSON and expose model, reasoning effort, cost, context, duration, and token information. Claude usage functions expose account-level utilization returned by the Anthropic usage endpoint. Codex status functions expose the configured model, reasoning effort, and elapsed session duration. When Codex has no explicit reasoning-effort override, the status helper reports Codex's default @samp{medium} effort. The session switcher uses backend busy, input-readiness, and background-work detectors to avoid showing actively responding sessions as waiting, and to distinguish truly idle waiting sessions from sessions that are accepting input while background terminals, agents, or tasks are still running. A session counts as waiting only when it is blocked on the user, meaning it has stopped and will not proceed until the user submits something. Being able to queue text mid-turn is not waiting, since both CLIs accept typed input at any point. Codex app-server sessions learn turn boundaries from the JSON-RPC stream, so an inactive turn on a live app server is authoritative for waiting and an active turn is authoritative for busy, regardless of whether the prompt accepts input. Terminal sessions have no such signal and fall back to the session-event state machine and to scanning rendered status text. Codex prompt submission clears the previous waiting marker at the target buffer, so a session does not remain colored as waiting after the user sends a new prompt.

@node Transient menu
@chapter Transient menu

@findex agent-menu
Backend modules extend @code{agent-menu} through @code{transient-append-suffix} after the core menu is available, first removing their own existing suffixes so reloading does not duplicate or stale-position those entries. The base menu contains shared session, tool, alert, buffer, prompt, and option entries, including the shared @code{agent-history} delegation to the optional Agent Log package. The Claude module appends branch navigation, batch TODOs, TODO sending, status polling, Claude account selection, and branch-kill warnings. The Codex module appends Codex resume/fork commands and Codex account selection. In the Options group, @samp{-c} selects the Claude account and @samp{-x} selects the Codex account so the two account selectors appear next to each other. Both account infixes share one implementation in @samp{agent-account.el}, and both sync the selected account's config home as soon as the selection is made.

Boolean menu infixes use a shared transient class that toggles the bound variable each time it is invoked. The theme-sync infix additionally calls @code{agent-sync-theme-now} when enabling theme sync, so backend configuration files are updated immediately.

